(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["microbiology-exam-2-epidemiology-quiz"]={"title":"Epidemiology — Quiz 1","category":"Microbiology","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which three elements form the basic triad of descriptive epidemiology?","choices":["Who, what and why","Incidence, prevalence and mortality","Person, place and time","Host, agent and environment"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: person, place and time are the essential characteristics of a descriptive study, understood before any analytic study is undertaken. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Researchers compare past exposures in people who have a disease with those in people who do not, to test whether the exposure causes the disease. Which branch of epidemiology is this?","choices":["Analytic","Descriptive","Syndromic","Sentinel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: relating an exposure to an outcome to test a cause-effect hypothesis is analytic epidemiology; the case-control study is one of its typical designs. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"What did the 1950 study by Doll and Hill link?","choices":["Water supply and cholera","Diet and heart disease","Lice and epidemic typhus","Smoking and lung cancer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Doll and Hill, 1950, linked smoking and lung cancer, an early application of epidemiology to behavior-related health outcomes. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Which of Hill's criteria requires that exposure precede disease?","choices":["Temporal sequence","Biological gradient","Consistency","Specificity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: temporal sequence requires that exposure precede disease; with a latency period, exposure must precede the latent period. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 46 and 50"},{"q":"Evaluating vaccine and clinical trials, and managing the staff and resources they need, belongs to which use of epidemiological training?","choices":["Program evaluation and management","Outbreak response","Training and mentoring","Public health communication"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: program evaluation and management covers evaluating prevention and intervention programs, such as vaccine and clinical trials, and managing their staff and resources. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"Where is cancer reported?","choices":["It is not reportable","To state cancer registries","By telephone to the state","As a yearly total of cases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: cancer is reported to state cancer registries, its own group among the reportable conditions. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 22 and 23"},{"q":"Which type of epidemic is often zoonotic or vector-borne?","choices":["Other","Propagated","Common source, point","Mixed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the category 'other' covers epidemics that are often zoonotic or vector-borne, such as Lyme disease and West Nile encephalitis. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 55 and image on Slide 58"},{"q":"A county counts residents newly diagnosed with type 2 diabetes during the past year and divides by the county's residents at midyear. What is it measuring?","choices":["Prevalence","Cause-specific mortality","Case fatality rate","Incidence"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: new cases arising during a given period, over the population, is incidence; the numerator of incidence is new cases only. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 27 and 28"},{"q":"Morbidity in a population is measured by which two measures?","choices":["Basic and effective reproduction","Incidence and prevalence","Crude and infant mortality","Sensitivity and specificity"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: morbidity is measured by incidence (new cases over a period) and prevalence (all cases present over a period). — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Which measure was part of the population approach to youth smoking?","choices":["Higher taxes on tobacco","Lower taxes on tobacco","More tobacco advertising","Easier youth access to tobacco"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the measures included eliminating tobacco smoke in public places, decreasing advertising and promotion, decreasing youth access to tobacco and increasing taxes on tobacco. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Besides vaccination, what else adds to herd immunity?","choices":["Lowering the case fatality rate","Recovery from the infection","Treating cases with antibiotics","Reporting cases to authorities"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: herd immunity is often discussed with vaccination, and it is also impacted by natural immunity gained by recovery from the infection. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"What is the prevailing wisdom in epidemiology about judgments of cause and effect?","choices":["One strong study settles them","They rest on association alone","They are tentative and open to new evidence","They are final once a study is published"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: most judgments of cause and effect are tentative and are open to change with new evidence. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 43 and 53"},{"q":"What is genomic epidemiology?","choices":["Mapping cases by home and workplace","Monitoring the genetic makeup of pathogens","Tracing inherited disease in families","Testing new vaccines in clinical trials"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: genomic epidemiology is the process of monitoring the genetic makeup of the pathogens that cause disease, made possible by advances in sequencing. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 12 and 69"},{"q":"In the person-place-time triad, which of these is a 'person' characteristic?","choices":["Climate and humidity","Urban or rural setting","Seasonal pattern","Socioeconomic status"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: person characteristics include age, gender, socioeconomic status (education, occupation, income), marital status, ethnicity or genetic profile, and behavior. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 34, 35 and 36"},{"q":"Which mortality rate is used to compare health status between countries?","choices":["Infant mortality rate","Crude mortality rate","Cause-specific mortality rate","Case fatality rate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: the infant mortality rate, the number of infant deaths, is used to compare health status between countries. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"Where does case surveillance begin?","choices":["The Centers for Disease Control and Prevention","Hospital billing offices","Community health surveys","Local and state health departments"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: case surveillance begins with local and state public health departments collecting data about cases; notifiable conditions are then reported on to the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A relative risk that is either very high or very low is less likely to be due to chance or bias. Which of Hill's criteria is this?","choices":["Consistency of findings","Biological gradient","Temporal sequence","Strength of association"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: a strong association is one in which the relative risk is either very high or very low, and it is less likely to be caused by chance or bias. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 46 and 48"},{"q":"A surveillance system counts cases of diarrhea rather than laboratory-confirmed cholera. Which type of surveillance is this?","choices":["Active surveillance","Genomic surveillance","Syndromic surveillance","Sentinel surveillance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: syndromic surveillance uses case definitions based entirely on clinical features, such as diarrhea instead of cholera; it is the system most often found in developing countries. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"What is the first step in investigating a communicable disease outbreak?","choices":["Develop a focused hypothesis","Implement control measures","Establish its existence","Verify the diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the investigation begins by establishing the existence of an outbreak, then preparing for field work and verifying the diagnosis. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"In John Snow's analytic study, houses supplied by the company that drew its water from where had far more cholera deaths?","choices":["The Thames upstream of London","A deep brewery well","Rainwater cisterns","The Thames downstream of London"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: Snow's hypothesis was that water from below London carried cholera; houses supplied by the Southwark and Vauxhall Company, still drawing downstream, had a far higher death rate than Lambeth houses. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"Air-quality ratings collected by sensors are which source of epidemiologic data?","choices":["Nonhealth-related sources","The environment","Individual persons","Health care providers"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the environment supplies data through samples and sensors for environmental changes, including air-quality ratings. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which term describes a disease that occurs at irregular intervals or as isolated incidents?","choices":["Endemic","Epidemic","Pandemic","Sporadic"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: sporadic diseases occur at irregular intervals or as isolated incidents. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Which critical characteristic of surveillance allows individual persons with disease to be identified for treatment or quarantine?","choices":["Timeliness","Representation","Sensitivity","Specificity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: sensitivity allows identification of individual persons with disease, to facilitate treatment, quarantine or other control measures. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A study compares illness among residents living near a hazardous waste disposal site with residents living elsewhere. Which application of epidemiology is this?","choices":["Social and behavioral epidemiology","Policy development","Environmental epidemiology","Occupational health"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: environmental epidemiology studies how environmental factors affect health, including exposure to air and water pollution or to hazardous waste disposal sites. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"An epidemiologist describes who gets a disease, who dies from it and what its outcome is, to rank it against other diseases of concern. Which use of epidemiological training is this?","choices":["Disease prioritization","Outbreak response","Public health communication","Experimental epidemiology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: disease prioritization evaluates and ranks diseases of concern, including describing the clinical picture: who gets the disease, who dies and what the outcome is. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 7 and 8"},{"q":"Taken from its Greek roots, epidemiology is the science of what?","choices":["What grows in the laboratory","What falls upon people","What lives inside people","What spreads between animals"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: epi means upon, demos means people and ology means science, so epidemiology is the science that deals with what falls upon people. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Why did the health department's response to the first Legionnaires' disease cases in 1976 start late?","choices":["The department was closed for the weekend","The disease was not yet reportable","Doctors refused to report the cases","The cases were outside the country"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: doctors with the first cases tried to contact the state health department, but it was closed for the weekend, and by Monday 18 deaths had occurred. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which type of surveillance uses staff members to contact health care providers regularly for data?","choices":["Syndromic surveillance","Sentinel surveillance","Active surveillance","Passive surveillance"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: active surveillance uses staff to regularly contact providers; it is the most accurate but the most expensive, and often used during outbreaks or seasonal events. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 19 and 20"},{"q":"In public health, interventions are most often designed on the basis of what?","choices":["Genomic sequences","Associations","Proven causes","Single case reports"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: most often, in public health, interventions are designed on the basis of associations; causation is inferred, not usually proven first. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What makes a disease reportable (notifiable)?","choices":["It is usually fatal","Law requires its reporting","It is caused by a bacterium","It has no available treatment"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: a reportable (notifiable) disease is one required by law to be reported to government authorities. — Lecture 12 Dr. Webster - Epidemiology.pptx, Slide 21"}]};
